===Etymology===
{{ety|en|:inh|enm:welcome|tree=+}}
From {{inh|en|enm|welcome}}, {{m|enm|wolcume}}, {{m|enm|wulcume}}, {{m|enm|wilcume}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wilcuma}} {{gloss|“a wished-for guest”; compare also {{m|ang|wilcume||welcome!|pos=interjection}}}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*willjakwemō}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wiljakwemô|t=a wished-for arrival or guest}}, possibly from {{m|gem-pro|*wiljakwemaną|t=to be welcome}}, equivalent to {{com|en|will|t1=desire|come|t2=comer, arrival}}. The component ''wil-'' was replaced by ''wel-'' when the sense “guest” of the second component was no longer understood, likely under influence from the adverb {{m|en|well}}. 
{{col-top|2|cog}}
Cognate with {{cog|sco|walcome}}, {{cog|yol|welcome}}, {{m|yol|welkome}}, {{cog|frr|welkimen}}, {{cog|stq|wilkemen}}, {{m|stq|wäilkeemen}}, {{m|stq|wülkemen}}, {{cog|fy|wolkom}}, {{cog|gsw|wol chomne}}, {{m|gsw|wolgcheemen}}, {{m|gsw|woul chemne}}, {{m|gsw|wéllkòmm}}, {{cog|cim|bóolkhèmm}}, {{cog|nl|welkom}} (earlier ''willecome''), {{cog|de|willkommen}}, {{cog|nds-de|willkamen}}, {{cog|li|welkom}}, {{m|li|wéllekemm}}, {{cog|lb|wëllkomm}}, {{cog|da,nb|velkommen}}, {{cog|ovd|welkumin}}, {{cog|fo|vælkomin}}, {{cog|is|velkominn}}, {{cog|nn|velkomen}}, {{cog|sv|välkommen}}, and {{cog|fro|wilecome}} (whence {{cog|frm|willecomme|t=welcome}}), from Germanic.

The verb is from {{inh|en|enm|welcomen}}, {{m|enm|wolcumen}}, {{m|enm|wilcumen}}, from {{inh|en|ang|wellcumian}}, {{m|ang|wylcumian}}, {{m|ang|wilcumian|t=to welcome, receive gladly}}.

Similar constructions are found in Modern {{ncog|el|καλώς ορίσατε}}, [[South Slavic]] languages, such as {{ncog|bg|добре́ дошъ́л }}, {{ncog|sh|dobrodošao}}, 
and also in [[Romance language]]s, such as {{ncog|it|benvenuto}}, {{ncog|es|bienvenido}},  {{ncog|fr|bienvenu}}, {{ncog|ca|benvingut}}, {{ncog|pt|bem-vindo}} and {{ncog|ro|bun venit}}, meaning “[may you have fared] well [in] coming [here]”. These Romance terms do not derive from a Classical Latin root, as no similar construction in Latin is found to exist, but are instead presumed (considering the ruling elite of the Germanic kingdoms which succeeded the {{w|Western Roman Empire}}) to be the result of a calque from a Germanic language into Proto-Romance (Vulgar Latin; see {{cog|la|*bene venūtus}}, and compare {{m|la|perdōnō}} and {{m|la|compāniō}} for similar historical calques).
{{col-bottom}}

